WEBVTT

NOTE Narrated by a synthetic voice (an ElevenLabs stock voice); the opening disclosure is the macOS Trinoids voice. Text by Phil Renato.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.179
Fertilization.

00:00:03.179 --> 00:00:04.758
(Plastic Sex)

00:00:05.758 --> 00:00:10.655
“Many a page of prose and many a narrative have been devoted to expressing

00:00:10.655 --> 00:00:15.820
what was, in effect, a sob, a moan, a laugh, or a piercing scream. The written

00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:20.918
word spells out in sequence what is quick and implicit in the spoken word.” —

00:00:20.918 --> 00:00:22.058
Marshall McLuhan.

00:00:22.058 --> 00:00:27.252
In the beginning there was sound cascading through plasma (with peaks that

00:00:27.252 --> 00:00:32.732
were 400,000 light years apart.) As it cooled it became man, and our ancestors

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:38.068
moaned and grunted. A shadow appeared overhead, made the hair on the back of

00:00:38.068 --> 00:00:43.761
our necks stand up and we used our lungs to pump air past tracheas to vibrate the

00:00:43.761 --> 00:00:48.742
larynx, creating echoes through the throat, mouth and nose. We did this

00:00:48.742 --> 00:00:53.794
automatically, without conscious control — and then, eventually, because

00:00:53.794 --> 00:00:58.704
exercising and governing this muscle to make reconfigurable sounds was

00:00:58.704 --> 00:01:03.685
advantageous for teaching and learning. We increasingly specialized our

00:01:03.685 --> 00:01:09.306
soundmaking, developing dictions of what we needed and wanted. And we shouted it

00:01:09.306 --> 00:01:12.864
in song. What can be shown and sung cannot be said.

00:01:12.864 --> 00:01:19.140
“I can’t say it like I sing it, I can’t sing it like I think it and I can’t think

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:20.524
it like I feel it.”.

00:01:20.524 --> 00:01:29.533
Footnote 14. Pedro the Lion, David Bazan, The Fleecing, 2004.

00:01:29.533 --> 00:01:34.765
The first stories were lullabies, evolving from a primitive hum. We calmed

00:01:34.765 --> 00:01:40.284
our children and sung to our sick while their lives drained into early graves.

00:01:40.284 --> 00:01:46.162
There was variation—as we spread from one valley to another; but the oratorios came

00:01:46.162 --> 00:01:51.537
and adapted with us. We drank with our hands, painted our bodies with earth,

00:01:51.537 --> 00:01:57.200
strung shells around our necks, carved wood with chipped stone, developed clays,

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.790
smithed metal and manipulated fibers to increasingly dense soundtracks. We made

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:04.224
drums before swords.

00:02:04.224 --> 00:02:09.901
The technology of speech divided us from our intuition, augmenting our conscious

00:02:09.901 --> 00:02:14.859
minds while slightly dulling the reactions in our subconscious spines.

00:02:14.859 --> 00:02:20.477
But this was all made from hydrogen and carbon; our mutation into a verbal and

00:02:20.477 --> 00:02:26.021
then a literate species was organic. We are not capable of the unnatural. But

00:02:26.021 --> 00:02:31.347
that lassez-faire summary subverts the words we expended so much energy to

00:02:31.347 --> 00:02:36.819
develop, things we committed to marble. Our gouged characters, morphing from

00:02:36.819 --> 00:02:42.291
chaos into standardized alphabets, stole nuanced melody and replaced it with

00:02:42.291 --> 00:02:47.325
precise notes. By the time we were transforming sand into glass we had

00:02:47.325 --> 00:02:52.942
ablated what had been our sole means of abstract communication—we were able to

00:02:52.942 --> 00:02:58.487
see through speech into the past and to project ideas into the future without

00:02:58.487 --> 00:03:02.135
building. Our temples were “mathematics in stone.”.

00:03:02.135 --> 00:03:07.465
When I first read McLuhan, some eighteen years after his death, I had trouble. His

00:03:07.465 --> 00:03:12.335
waves were so strong that they kept pushing me back to shore. I finally got

00:03:12.335 --> 00:03:17.139
the hang of him in a chapter called The Written Word, and specifically his

00:03:17.139 --> 00:03:18.389
suggestion that we:

00:03:18.389 --> 00:03:23.609
“Suppose that, instead of displaying the Stars and Stripes, we were to write the

00:03:23.609 --> 00:03:28.565
words “American flag” across a piece of cloth and to display that. While the

00:03:28.565 --> 00:03:33.653
symbols would convey the same meaning, the effect would be quite different. To

00:03:33.653 --> 00:03:38.939
translate the rich visual mosaic of the Stars and Stripes into written form would

00:03:38.939 --> 00:03:44.357
be to deprive it of most of its qualities of corporate image and of experience, yet

00:03:44.357 --> 00:03:47.925
the abstract literal bond would remain much the same.”.

00:03:47.925 --> 00:03:53.237
McLuhan uses this to illustrate a change from ‘tribal to literate;’ a change that

00:03:53.237 --> 00:03:58.483
allows individuals to live without close social groups due to the portability of

00:03:58.483 --> 00:04:03.796
the written word. I was struggling with more than his vocabulary and grammar when

00:04:03.796 --> 00:04:08.909
I first read this passage; with various tensions between knowledge and living,

00:04:08.909 --> 00:04:13.557
hypothesis and synthesis, ideas, and objects. McLuhan took considerable

00:04:13.557 --> 00:04:17.940
scholastic liberties with his theories—but it wasn’t the truth in a

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:23.119
global sense that made them true, it was the way they lined up with the world I

00:04:23.119 --> 00:04:23.916
experienced.

00:04:23.916 --> 00:04:28.778
I cried, heard, watched, sang, talked, walked, read, rode in pretty much the

00:04:28.778 --> 00:04:33.770
same order as everyone else. But I didn’t crawl. My mother isn’t sure why, and

00:04:33.770 --> 00:04:38.891
nothing much was ever made of it—because I never had a diagnosed condition where

00:04:38.891 --> 00:04:43.947
this could be a cause. But my guess is that it has to do with what we can reach

00:04:43.947 --> 00:04:48.874
in each of these positions. What I wanted most was too high to reach from the

00:04:48.874 --> 00:04:53.218
floor—so I think I learned to walk because that got me closer to the

00:04:53.218 --> 00:04:57.950
pacifier and the sugar bowl. I don’t remember the first book I read, but I

00:04:57.950 --> 00:05:03.136
remember the yellow latex nipple that was in my mouth in the few moments I wasn’t

00:05:03.136 --> 00:05:08.257
eating or speaking. And because it had been dipped in granulated sucrose, bursts

00:05:08.257 --> 00:05:13.184
of mania would often grip me within moments after putting it into my mouth in

00:05:13.184 --> 00:05:18.305
a cycle of suckling, spitting, speaking, screaming, suckling… Sugar is made from

00:05:18.305 --> 00:05:23.362
beets and cane, sliced and chopped to squeeze out juices, which get boiled down

00:05:23.362 --> 00:05:28.548
to crystals and molasses in a syrup. As they settle they are spun apart—the white

00:05:28.548 --> 00:05:33.734
from the brown—with the aid of char from the burned bones of slaughtered animals.

00:05:33.734 --> 00:05:38.531
The white crystals are dried, some pressed into cubes and packed into boxes

00:05:38.531 --> 00:05:43.717
that are poured into packets, into baking pans and mixed with almost every modern

00:05:43.717 --> 00:05:48.644
food. But some is left for the table, with metal spouts, ready to sweeten the

00:05:48.644 --> 00:05:49.876
already saccharine.

00:05:49.876 --> 00:05:54.962
Kaolinite, feldspar, quartz (or other silica bearing materials,) bones,

00:05:54.962 --> 00:06:00.339
alabaster, mullites, and/or other minerals are blunged with water to make a

00:06:00.339 --> 00:06:06.298
porcelain mud. This slip is poured into a two or more part plaster mold. The excess

00:06:06.298 --> 00:06:11.893
is dumped out, to be poured in again when the gypsum in the mold draws out the

00:06:11.893 --> 00:06:17.633
water from the last pour. After the mold is opened, the hollow vessel is removed

00:06:17.633 --> 00:06:23.374
and fettled with a special knife, allowed to dry, and fired into a glass through

00:06:23.374 --> 00:06:28.896
vitrification. My grandmother’s china sugar bowl was screen printed with blue

00:06:28.896 --> 00:06:29.768
butterflies.

00:06:29.768 --> 00:06:35.225
A worker incises the outer layers of the hevea brasiliensis in a

00:06:35.225 --> 00:06:41.056
diagonal line (with a curved knife) in the early morning, letting loose a white

00:06:41.056 --> 00:06:46.962
milk from its laticifers. These common, largely defensive aqueous polymers, flow

00:06:46.962 --> 00:06:52.719
down the helical score in the bark of the tree into a tapping cup in darkness.

00:06:52.719 --> 00:06:58.325
These cups fill buckets, buckets fill barrels, and barrels fill tanks. Tanks

00:06:58.325 --> 00:07:04.231
empty into vats, are creamed, and made into liquid concentrates that are shipped

00:07:04.231 --> 00:07:09.838
to become sheets and slabs, to be lakes that ceramic hands and phalluses are

00:07:09.838 --> 00:07:15.968
dipped into. All this flows into ovens or molds that vulcanize the latex into final

00:07:15.968 --> 00:07:18.809
forms—most importantly: proxy nipples.

00:07:18.809 --> 00:07:24.160
I was her favorite grandchild; something that chafes cousins to this day. I sat in

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:29.181
her living room, in an antique school chair, for much of my childhood—reading

00:07:29.181 --> 00:07:34.135
or drawing. Listening to her husband watch The Price is Right, watching beer

00:07:34.135 --> 00:07:38.825
cans (clad with latex neoprene insulators) empty. I whined every time my

00:07:38.825 --> 00:07:43.780
book was boring, my paper was full, or my pacifier was cleaned of its simple

00:07:43.780 --> 00:07:48.735
carbohydrate. My skinny brother got no such treatment. He wasn’t ignored, he

00:07:48.735 --> 00:07:53.689
just wasn’t hovered over. Which meant he spent more time outside, practicing

00:07:53.689 --> 00:07:59.106
hitting balls that he had to run after in the yard. Whenever I ventured out to play

00:07:59.106 --> 00:08:04.325
with him, I spent more time wondering how a hollow plastic bat was formed with a

00:08:04.325 --> 00:08:09.147
wood texture, or eventually, how an aluminum bat was extruded with such an

00:08:09.147 --> 00:08:13.904
odd taper. And how were the graphics applied on a curved surface? And the

00:08:13.904 --> 00:08:19.255
handle felt solid—it couldn’t be made out of the same blank of material as the bat

00:08:19.255 --> 00:08:24.011
itself, right? “I’m sick of playing, I’ll be inside” (sucking on sugar.).

00:08:24.011 --> 00:08:29.365
Five years after my grandmother died, I sat in a dark room, watching an animated

00:08:29.365 --> 00:08:34.856
college professor point to figurines that were projected from color reverse slides

00:08:34.856 --> 00:08:40.278
onto a large, white screen in Ford Hall. These handheld stone and ceramic figures

00:08:40.278 --> 00:08:45.564
transfixed me; not only were they among the first objects to be made that we’ve

00:08:45.564 --> 00:08:50.580
found, they utterly transformed the materials for purposes that we can only

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:55.731
speculate on. Larger pieces of jet, hematite, bone, and ivory were reduced to

00:08:55.731 --> 00:09:00.543
smaller forms. Clay bodies were permanently changed from mud to neostone

00:09:00.543 --> 00:09:05.830
through heating. And women were turned, through observation and reconstruction,

00:09:05.830 --> 00:09:11.185
into stupid mutant gods with tiny heads and legs, with huge bellys, breasts, and

00:09:11.185 --> 00:09:15.726
vulvas. While some show evidence of having been tied as if made into

00:09:15.726 --> 00:09:21.080
pendants, or hung on the left sides of huts, many ended up being rubbed with red

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:24.198
and yellow earth and destroyed in pits of fire.

00:09:24.198 --> 00:09:29.869
The Venus of Dolní Věstonice is the oldest known ceramic object that

00:09:29.869 --> 00:09:35.110
has been found, and nearly a century after being broken out of its shallow

00:09:35.110 --> 00:09:40.638
grave in Moravia it was tomographically scanned. As the penetrating waves that

00:09:40.638 --> 00:09:45.592
bounced off this hard protogod were reconstructed, researchers saw the

00:09:45.592 --> 00:09:51.407
reverse impressions of human fingers—made by an eleven year old child, who handled

00:09:51.407 --> 00:09:56.934
it before it was fired about twenty-five thousand years ago. While the maker’s

00:09:56.934 --> 00:10:02.032
name and biographical data is unknown, I am fairly sure we had different

00:10:02.032 --> 00:10:07.200
pre-adolescent experiences of very different worlds. While I was mouthing

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:12.513
rubber sugar tits and watching Magnum P.I.’s Ferrari 308GTS tear up Hawaii,

00:10:12.513 --> 00:10:17.969
they were practicing magic—making or tending to steroid breasted objects that

00:10:17.969 --> 00:10:22.061
would bring their people health, abundance, and fertility.

00:10:22.061 --> 00:10:26.943
We search. But not knowing, taking the risk of finding fault, is the point.

00:10:26.943 --> 00:10:31.560
Discovering laws that aren’t and investing them into the wrong forms is

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:36.376
far likelier than crafting the right. And this keeps our economy of errors

00:10:36.376 --> 00:10:40.730
constantly expanding. “It’s wanting to know that makes us matter.”.

